\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Deputy PM:Asked Ctrl Bk To Allow THB Move Naturally \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2003\par 11:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- Thai Deputy Prime Minister Somkid Jatusripitak said Tuesday that he has asked the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market while closely monitoring the baht's movements. \par \par \uc2  "The baht has strengthened because of capital inflows," Somkid told reporters. "I have asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and let the baht move naturally." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has admitted to "managing the exchange rate" to keep the baht stable. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Tuesday, Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said that the baht would have been even stronger if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't stepped into the market. \par \par \uc2  The baht hit a 10-month high against the U.S. dollar early Tuesday, at 41.63 to the dollar. At 0933 GMT, it was trading at THB41.71 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the Thai economy, with gross domestic product growth in the first quarter expected at more than 6%, has led foreign investors to shift their investments into Thailand's manufacturing sector and capital markets, said Somkid. \par \par \uc2  Strong export growth of more than 20% so far this year has also helped fuel Thai economic growth, he added. \par \par \uc2  Part of the capital inflow comes from Japanese investors, many of which are planning to move their manufacturing bases to Thailand, he said. \par \par \uc2  During a recent visit to Japan, Somkid said major Japanese automakers, including Mitsubishi Motors Corp. (J.MOT or 7211) and Honda Motor Co. (HMC), plan to increase their investments in Thailand to the tune of almost THB18 billion. \par \par \uc2  The baht is also supported by the dollar's overall weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Somkid noted. \par \par \uc2  Commerce Minister Adisai Bodharamik said earlier Tuesday that the dollar might soon drop to below THB40 but didn't give a specific timeframe. \par \par \uc2  -By Bangkok Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; djn.bangkok@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030527dz5r000l5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
