\par\par \b \uc2  Thai T-bill yields slip on strong baht, short supply. \b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2003\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, May 26 (Reuters) - Yields on three-month and six-month Thai treasury bills slipped to two-month lows at Monday's auction as the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened and investors expected supply of government debt to shrink. \par \par \uc2  The average accepted yield for three billion baht ($71.51 million) worth of 91-day treasury bills fell to 1.534 percent from 1.551 percent in a similar auction a week ago. Demand was lower at 3.73 times the amount on offer from 4.22 times. \par \par \uc2  The yield for three billion baht worth of 182-day treasury bills dipped to 1.611 percent from 1.614 percent last week. Demand eased to 2.85 times the amount on offer from 3.39 times. \par \par \uc2  "Yields are too low. The three-month should be around 1.62 and the six-month around 1.65," said a dealer at a foreign bank. The Thai government has been cutting its debt issues because its budget position has been improving on better-than-expected revenue collection. In May, Thailand sold only 24 billion baht of treasury bills, against 40 billion baht in April. \par \par \uc2  Investors are expecting government debt supply to shrink further, driving yields across the curve lower. \par \par \uc2  Thai Prime Minster Thaksin Shinawatra said last week that Thailand would notch up a surprise budget surplus of around 40 billion baht for the current fiscal year, against an expected budget deficit of 174 billion baht. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Thai baht's gain against the U.S. dollar was also boosting the appeal of Thai debt in recent weeks. The Thai baht has risen 2.9 percent against the dollar in the last month. \par \par \uc2  "The baht's strength is making investors want to hold more baht assets and less dollar assets," a trader at a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  By 0624 GMT, the Thai baht stood at 41.74 against the dollar, a high not seen since August 2002. \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying Thai exports were still competitive at the current baht level. ($1=41.95 baht). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030526dz5q00etn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
