\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends At 8-Mo High On Equity Related Inflows \b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 12:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended at an eight-month high against the dollar in Asia Wednesday driven by strong equity related inflows and stop-loss dollar selling by offshore players, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 42.22 baht, off its intraday low of THB42.18, but the U.S. unit was still trading at eight-month troughs. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at THB42.39 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit opened at the day's high of THB42.42 before strong dollar selling pulled the dollar down and weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for most of the local session. \par \par \uc2  "There was heavy dollar selling led by Hong Kong players and also some genuine dollar selling for equity related purposes today," said a foreign bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have been buying into Thai stocks recently, purchasing a net THB1.74 billion in shares over the past two days. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall triggered stop-loss sales as the U.S. unit breached key THB42.35 support and then THB42.30 on the way through, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said local banks, possibly on behalf of the Bank of Thailand, have been buying dollars Wednesday, but the amount wasn't significant. \par \par \uc2  "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to slowdown the baht's sharp gains during the day. Their buying wasn't enough to dictate the market," said a local bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to fall further when local trade resumes Friday, pegging key support for the dollar at THB42.00. Resistance is expected at THB42.30 on any move up. \par \par \uc2  Thai financial markets will be closed Thursday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Thai long-term government bond prices ended higher Wednesday, but off highs as a continuing rally on low upcoming supply and a low interest-rate environment stalled amid profit-taking ahead of Thursday's public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Dealer said investors will likely remain cautious Friday unless there was sharp movement in U.S. Treasurys during the Thai holiday to provide direction. \par \par \uc2  If any of the U.S. economic data to be issued Thursday raised expectations of a future cut in U.S. interest rates, the Thai bond rally could resume despite the usual caution ahead of the weekend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Long-term government bond yields ended just off new historic lows set in intraday trade. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the government bond due November 2012 ended in a bid-offer range of 2.98%-2.96%, up from 2.965%-2.955% in early trade but still down from Tuesday's close of 2.985%. \par \par \uc2  The issue due November 2022 was traded at 4.05%, down from an average accepted yield of 4.10% at an auction reopening the issue Tuesday and from 4.11% at Tuesday's secondary market close. \par \par \uc2  High financial system liquidity sustained demand in the short-end of the market, however. \par \par \uc2  One-year \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds ended in a bid-offer range of 1.63%-1.62%, down from trades at 1.64% early Wednesday and 1.67% at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Overnight money market interest rates closed flat despite inflows of baht liquidity from dollar selling due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowed baht from primary dealers through the seven-day repurchase, or repo, market term to absorb excess liquidity and to reactivate the term, which had fallen quiet Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Institutions found the seven-day repo unattractive since the rate was trapped at the same level as overnight rates, just under the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 14-day repurchase market target rate of 1.75%. But primary dealers had to respond to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's offers to sell securities under repurchase agreements. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term            Wednesday         Tuesday\par Interbank  Overnight/Late  1.68750%          1.68750%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close 1.68750%          1.68750%\par            14-Day          1.75000%          1.75000%\par            1-Month         no activity       no activity\par            3-Month         no activity       no activity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Suttinee Yuvejwattana and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266\par 0744; djn.bangkok@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Simon Harvey\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030514dz5e000t3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
