\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Up after Fed, fear  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2003\par 10:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 7 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gathered upward momentum on Wednesday after the U.S. Federal Reserve's warning of economic weakness drove the U.S. dollar to deeper lows, but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local central banks restricted their gains. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Korean won, Thai baht and Taiwan dollar all climbed to nearly two-month highs in morning deals, but then pared some gains. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso rose to an eight-month high of 52.0, extending its recent bull run after the country's tax agency reported tax collections in April exceeded the target, but then it also stalled. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. dollar downtrend is still pretty much entrenched," said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "I think the central banks are watching the markets, otherwise we'd see the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher." \par \par \uc2  Traders said they suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They also suspected the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had checked the peso around 52, and Korean authorities might have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars after warning against excessive strength in the won early on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities also repeated their resolve to act against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell to four-year lows against the euro and nearly two-month lows against the yen after the Fed left rates steady at Tuesday's meeting and warned of the risks to growth. It got some respite after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated support for the strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  SARS UNWOUND \par \par \uc2  The rise in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reflected the unwinding of trades centred around the risk from the Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) outbreak, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At its morning high of 1.7385/90, the Sing dollar was up three cents or two percent from Friday and had reversed the losses it incurred since mid-March, when the Iraq war began and the first local cases of SARS were detected. \par \par \uc2  Barclays Capital said the move might reflect a policy desire to have a neutral trade-weighted index. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that the Sing dollar's strength mainly reflects the MAS' desire to steer the Sing dollar back to a neutral monetary policy setting now that the SARS epidemic has been brought under control in Singapore," it said in a note. \par \par \uc2  BANK INDONESIA SURPRISES \par \par \uc2  The rupiah fluctuated between a 10-week high of 8,525 per dollar struck offshore and a low of 8,595, before ending near the highs which took its gains in two weeks to three percent. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Indonesia surprised markets with a statement hinting the gains were substantially due to short-term capital inflows seeking some of Asia's highest yielding papers. \par \par \uc2  "In regards to indications about the inflows of short-term funds...Bank Indonesia sees it as necessary to create a conducive climate to lure long-term funds," the bank said. It did not elaborate. \par \par \uc2  Traders had suspected foreign money for the privatisation of a majority stake in Bank Danamon had primarily boosted the rupiah, besides funds seeking the high yield in the country. \par \par \uc2  The baht's rise beyond 42.50 was fleeting. It stayed just a shade weaker than 42.50 with markets turning cautious after its 1.5 percent rise in under two weeks and amid talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won ended onshore trade at a two-month high of 1,199 per dollar, after coming off a 1,188 peak. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0827 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.48       118.40         +0.78\par   Sing dlr      1.7429       1.7442         +0.07\par   Taiwan dlr    34.712       34.812         +0.29\par   Korean won   1198.60      1203.90         +0.44\par   Baht           42.55        42.60         +0.12\par   Peso           52.07        52.30         +0.44\par   Rupiah       8530.00      8600.00         +0.82\par   Change on year\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.48       118.74         +1.07\par   Sing dlr      1.7429       1.7341         -0.50\par   Taiwan dlr    34.712       34.753         +0.12\par   Korean won   1198.60      1185.80         -1.07\par   Baht           42.55        43.08         +1.25\par   Peso           52.07        53.38         +2.52\par   Rupiah       8530.00      8940.00         +4.81.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030507dz5700ioe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
