\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Fears of war and central banks block gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 09:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The Thai baht edged closer to\par three-week highs on Thursday but, like most of its Asian\par counterparts, the risks of war in Iraq and fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par by authorities capped its gains.\par     The baht rose to around 42.65 per dollar, aiming for the\par three-week high of 42.60 hit in offshore trade on Tuesday, as a\par lack of dollar demand from importers triggered stop-loss dollar\par sell orders lined around 42.70.\par     "The dollar selling was mostly coming from offshore and\par some from local exporters and there wasn't much demand from\par importers, not as much as some were expecting," said a dealer\par in Bangkok.\par  \par     But the rally was cut short after dollar bids from some\par foreign names sparked rumours of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "The BOT (Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has been on the bid side for\par some days. But they aren't buying a lot of dollars. Maybe they\par just want to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stable," the dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Analysts said several central banks in the region have\par recently been attempting to douse \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength in a bid to\par avoid trade-weighted gains that erode export competitiveness.\par     "If the markets were completely liberal, most Asian\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be rallying at this point given the dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par weakness," said Peter Redward, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Deutsche\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The U.S. dollar eased to its lowest in almost six months\par against the yen and tested 1.08 against the euro.\par     Redward said with the dollar likely to soften further\par against other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would\par follow the trend but their gains would not be in lock step.\par     Apart from central banks' sensitivity, he said gains would\par also be tempered by the risks of war in Iraq and further sharp\par rise in oil prices.\par     In that environment, there was little incentive for traders\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to build significant positions in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the baht,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore dollar and Taiwan dollar, where domestic interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates were low.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE GDP\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That helped keep the Singapore dollar off three-week highs\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par around 1.7315 hit earlier in the week. It rose to around 1.7327\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par per U.S. dollar in early trade, before losing the momentum.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The gains were a knee-jerk response to news the economy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par grew an annualised 0.4 percent in the fourth quarter, exceeding\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par official advanced estimates of 0.1 percent, but still below\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market forecasts of 0.8 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There wasn't much of a response. I believe markets are not\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par looking at data at this moment," said Philip Wee, treasury\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market strategist at DBS Bank in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippines peso  was stuck in a tight 54.31-36\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par per dollar range, against Wednesday's onshore close of 54.35\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and two-year lows of 54.48 hit on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers in Manila said the peso was going through a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par technical correction after the steep fall to two-year lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Support came from offshore non-deliverable forwards (NDF)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market, where players were booking profits on the dollar's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sharp gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah eased to 8,900 per dollar, but fear\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of dollar sales from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept players from pushing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  beyond that level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pressure on the rupiah came from offshore players, who\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bought dollars in short-term tenors and pushed one-month NDFs\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par offers earlier in the day to 9,020 from below 9,000 earlier in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the week. The one-month NDF eased to 9,010 in late trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Korean won was slightly higher, but its upside was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par limited by recent losses in the local stock market and concerns\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par about the North Korean nuclear stand-off.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Taiwan dollar rose, aided by the yen, but month-end\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demand for the U.S. dollar from onshore players kept gains\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par limited.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0839 GMT\par  Change on the day\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     117.02       117.40         +0.32\par  Sing dlr      1.7346       1.7348         +0.01\par  Taiwan dlr    34.745       34.784         +0.11\par  Korean won   1187.00       1188.50         +0.12\par  Baht           42.59        42.75         +0.38\par  Peso           54.37        54.35         -0.03\par  Rupiah       8894.00      8888.00         -0.07\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     117.02       118.74         +1.47\par  Sing dlr      1.7346       1.7341         -0.03\par  Taiwan dlr    34.745       34.753         +0.02\par  Korean won   1187.00      1185.80         -0.10\par  Baht           42.59        43.08         +1.15\par  Peso           54.37        53.38         -1.81\par  Rupiah       8894.00      8940.00         +0.52.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030227dz2r00hlo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
