\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Caution On Iraq Report \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,001 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2003\par 11:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged higher Monday but caution reigned ahead of a key report later in the global session by United Nations weapons inspectors that could indicate if a war against Iraq is imminent. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended just a tad higher at a fresh-six month closing high, helped by month-end dollar sales by exporters. \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy helped support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above the 1,170 won a dollar level, market players said. Some dealers also suspected the government bought dollars, but others said it was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  After trading between KRW1,169.5 and KRW1,172.0, the dollar ended at KRW1,170.7, down from KRW1,170.8 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, an official at the ministry said the recent drop in the dollar/won rate is "excessive" and the government is concerned about recent moves in the market. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, the New Taiwan dollar and the Thai baht were also higher. The Indonesian rupiah was steady but the Philippine peso bucked the trend and weakened as the market prepared for increased demand for dollars from oil companies. \par \par \uc2  Overall volume in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was thin as fears of a U.S.-led war in Iraq kept many market players sidelined or reluctant to take strong positions. \par \par \uc2  Some observers also attributed the low volumes to reduced trading interest ahead of the long Chinese Lunar New Year weekend. \par \par \uc2  "There isn't much appetite to take major directional views," said Vincent Low, Merrill Lynch's Singapore-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond strategy for Asia. \par \par \uc2  Monday's report by the Iraq weapons inspectors to the U.N. Security Council will be followed by President George W. Bush's State of the Union address Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Their comments are expected to signal if war is imminent. \par \par \uc2  Also Tuesday, market players will be keeping a close eye on Treasury Secretary nominee John Snow's confirmation hearing to see if he says anything about the U.S.' strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The near-term picture remains one of a weak U.S. dollar, and Asian central banks are likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help stem the decline, Low said. \par \par \uc2  But given the fact that there are structural reasons, not just a war risk premium, for the dollar's weakness, regional monetary authorities may have a dilemma in continuing to accumulate dollars aggressively, he added. \par \par \uc2  Still, with regional economies still in the nascent stages of recovery, their central banks are unlikely to want their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate too rapidly versus the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Low said although the broad view for the region remains one of a gradual economic recovery and a general resilience to geopolitical tension, "with Asia so leveraged to world growth," the region can't be insulated from major developments in the U.S., the world's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  At 0943 GMT, the dollar was quoted around Y117.66, down from Y117.72 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y117.96 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's better-than-expected industrial output data for December didn't generate much excitement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The local dollar's strength was due to the weak outlook for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted around S$1.7278 near the end of the local trading session, below S$1.7310 late Friday, but up from S$1.7235 - its lowest since late-2000 - in New York trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's industrial production rose 19% on year in December, beating the average 8.9% forecast predicted in a Dow Jones Newswires poll. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's strong export data had little impact on the Thai baht, which firmed as market players unloaded dollars on fears of a war in Iraq, though the market was wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying dollars on weakness to build foreign reserves and limit volatility. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished the local trading session at 42.60 baht, down from THB42.68 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Monday, the Thai Commerce Ministry Monday said exports rose 5.7% to $68.9 billion in 2002, while imports rose 4.1% to $64.3 billion. \par \par \uc2  But the ministry said exports growth will slow to 3% to 5% this year, due to uncertainties linked to Iraq and the possibility of a slowdown in the global economy. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar strengthened as fears of a war in Iraq encouraged selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Exporters and interbank players sold U.S. dollars during the day, though there were some importers who were dollar buyers. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought small amounts of U.S. dollars to slow the local unit's rise, but the buying wasn't very aggressive. \par \par \uc2  After moving between NT$34.510 and NT$34.568, the dollar closed at NT$34.560, down from NT$34.575 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar ended at 53.670 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.590 Friday, after moving between PHP53.575 and PHP53.685. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had opened weaker amid a lack of corporate demand, but gained strength in late trading as banks bought dollars. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody wanted to go short because we think the oil companies will be buying in the coming sessions and it might be a big demand," a trader with a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine government last week required oil companies to maintain higher inventories to prepare for possible supply disruptions in case war erupts in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the dollar closed unchanged at 8,893 rupiah after trading between IDR8,887 and IDR8,895. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar sales by local banks offset dollar purchases by offshore investors fearful of rising anti-Western sentiment arising from tensions over Iraq. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030127dz1r000mn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
