\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Higher Late;Won,NT$ At 2-Mo High \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2002\par 11:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed as the dollar lost ground against major rivals, particularly the yen, amid concerns about a U.S.-Iraq conflict and uncertainty about the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Regional units were also beneficiaries of the hunt for higher yields after the U.S. Federal Reserve's larger-than-expected 50 basis point rate cut last week. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar ended the day at two month highs, while the Thai baht hit levels unseen since September. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was hardly dented by Trade and Industry Minister George Yeo's comments that he isn't ruling out the possibility of a double-dip recession for the city state. \par \par \uc2  For the most part, trading ranges were tight and there wasn't much significant position-taking ahead of a U.S. holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  Market players were also waiting for Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan's views on the economy when he testifies before the Joint Economic Committee of Congress Wednesday, as well as a slew of U.S. economic data to be released this week. \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese government officials failed to curb the yen's strength in Asia. Nor did weak share prices and shaky economic data; the Nikkei 225 Stock Average briefly fell below its 19-year low during the session. \par \par \uc2  David Simmonds, senior emerging markets strategist for Asia at The Royal Bank of Scotland, said markets remain concerned about why the Fed cut rates so sharply last week. \par \par \uc2  Some wonder if the decision reflects a greater worry about the economy than anticipated earlier. \par \par \uc2  Markets are also refocusing on Iraq after the United Nations approved resolutions that calls on the country to eliminate its weapons of mass destruction or face "serious consequences. \par \par \uc2  For now, though, markets are waiting to see how Iraq's response, and how the U.S. interprets that response, Simmonds said. \par \par \uc2  If there is conflict and oil prices jump, it would further threaten Asian economies that are already grappling with a slowdown in the U.S., he said. That should also curtail regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' current strength, he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,201.0 won, down from KRW1,207.4 Friday, and its weakest finish since Sept. 10. Intraday, it traded between KRW1,197.8 and KRW1,203.3. \par \par \uc2  Only demand from importers helped lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back above KRW1,200 in the afternoon, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at NT$34.415, up from NT$34.505 Friday, and its lowest since Sept. 13, though bids from importers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped cushion the fall. \par \par \uc2  After the market's close, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a 25 basis point rate cut, a move widely expected by analysts since the Fed slashed rates last week. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted around S$1.7566 as of 1045 GMT, down from S$1.7615 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened at around S$1.7580 in Asia and slipped to S$1.7570 in the wake of its fall below 120 yen; it spent much of the session locked between S$1.7575 and S$1.7585. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said Yeo's bearish comments on the economy weren't a major surprise as most have already been expecting a weak economic performance in the second half. The government is widely expected to cut its full-year growth forecasts to 2%-3%, from 3%-4% currently. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar was quoted around 42.96 versus the Thai baht as of 1047 GMT, down from THB43.12 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  An Asian bank trader in Singapore said \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via agent banks to curb the baht's rise when the dollar slipped to an intraday trough of THB42.94, it's lowest since Sept. 19. \par \par \uc2  "But with the U.S. holiday (Monday), nobody is taking new positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Indonesian rupiah rose as offshore market players continued to liquidate long dollar positions as the probe into last month's deadly bombings in Bali progressed. \par \par \uc2  News that the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency sealed a pact Friday to sell a 51% stake in Bank Niaga to Malaysia's Commerce Asset-Holding Bhd. for around $114 million also lifted the market, dealers said. The deal is expected to bring capital flows into Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 9,145 rupiah, down from IDR9,190 Friday. \par \par \uc2  But the Philippine peso surrendered early gains as corporate demand for dollars emerged. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at its intraday high of PHP52.960 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP52.880 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's closing level was different from the PHP52.910 initially posted on the system. \par \par \uc2  Metropolitan Bank & Trust Co., the electronic exchange's officer of the week, said the discrepancy was due to late mapping of the last transaction executed on the dealing system. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the revision shouldn't stir negative reaction from market players since such incidents had happened in the past, and weren't an attempt to engineer the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021111dybb000lz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
