\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Higher Vs Dlr In Thin Trade, Tracks Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2002\par 11:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended at its day's high against the dollar in Asian trade Tuesday, boosted by an overnight rise in the Japanese yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 43.35 baht, down from THB43.53 at the Asian close Monday. \par \par \uc2  After stumbling against the yen overnight, the dollar opened at THB43.41 early in Asia before steadily losing more ground during the day due to offshore dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  However, trading in regional markets was fairly thin, keeping the dollar/baht in a tight range. \par \par \uc2  "They were some dollar buying orders from oil companies, but they couldn't help cap losses much," said a trader at foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers also suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the day to help limit the dollar's losses, citing dollar orders at THB43.34-THB43.35 by offshore banks that usually act on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bullish economic forecast by the National Economic & Social Development Board had no impact on the dollar/baht as the figures were somewhat in line with market expectations, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  NESDB, a state planning agency responsible for collecting production data, said Tuesday that Thailand's gross domestic product growth in the third quarter of 2002 is likely to be no less than 5.5% above the same period a year ago, supported by strong exports and local demand. \par \par \uc2  Growth for the full year is likely to be near the top end of a forecast range of 4% to 4.5%, possibly at 4.3%. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is expected to open in Asia Wednesday in a range of THB43.30-THB43.40, subject to the yen's movement overnight. \par \par \uc2  Thai government bond prices rose further Tuesday, on growing expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve Open Market Committee may cut interest rates at its Nov. 6 meeting, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Trading in the bond market was more active compared with Monday, as the Thai bourse ended its long-winning streak Tuesday, falling 1% at 353.95 points. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the government bond due April 2010 issue finished in a bid/offer range of 3.67%/3.66%, compared with 3.74% at Monday's close, while the March 2011 issue finished at 3.77%/3.76% versus 3.84% at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Traders said mutual funds were active buyers as they speculated that yields on long-dated papers may fall further Wednesday when a new 10-year government bond is auctioned. \par \par \uc2  "The upcoming auction of the 10-year bond helped spiced up the market activity as it's quite a novelty," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  The overnight interest rate finished flat despite end-of-month demand for cash, as liquidity remains ample, and pressure from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap rates eased given the stronger baht. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate is expected to ease when liquidity returns to the system at the start of November, dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term                   Tuesday      Monday\par Interbank Overnight/Most Active   1.87500%    1.87500%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close        1.71875%    1.71875%\par            14-Day                 2.00000%    2.00000%\par            1-Month                2.00000%    2.00000%\par            3-Month              no activity  no activity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 662-266-0744; rattaphol.onsanit@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021029dyat000od