\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Down; Hits 6-Mo Low On Offshore Dlr Buying \b0\par\par\uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2002\par 12:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended just off a six-month low against the dollar in Asian spot trade Monday, thanks to selling by offshore players as the dollar rallied against the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 43.78 baht, off a six-month high of THB43.80 but up sharply from THB43.51 at the Asian close Friday, despite two apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit was hovering at an intraday low around THB43.54 at the Asian open before a run up fueled by offshore players' adjustments to keep the baht in line with regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and local oil importers' demand. \par \par \uc2  Oil importers bought dollars as global oil prices rose following an explosion on an oil tanker off the coast of Yemen, which sparked a jump in Brent crude futures. Higher oil prices tend to weaken the baht because the country is a net importer. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's steep rise against the yen also convinced offshore players to buy the greenback. Most of the active players were European banks," a local bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit the baht's fall failed to stem the slide, as the U.S. unit rose a rising tide against the yen, which is the regional trendsetter. \par \par \uc2  "A Japanese bank in Singapore, which usually acts on behalf of BOT (Bank of Thailand) bought the baht two times at THB43.62 and THB43.72. But the orders were too weak to make much difference," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar convincingly pushed past initial resistance just above THB43.60, which held against several attempted breaches last week, more offshore and onshore players joined the dollar-buying binge. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit touched an intraday high of THB43.80 in late afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected the dollar to open Tuesday in Asia between THB43.60 and THB43.90, subject to any further volatility in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar-yen (exchange rate) is able to rise firmly above Y124 resistance by early Tuesday, the dollar-baht will likely open higher than THB43.80," a foreign bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Thai government bonds finished steady Monday as liquidity driven buying from local banks was offset by profit-taking by some foreign banks, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Trade was very thin on a lack of participation from long-term investors and a dearth of fresh leads. \par \par \uc2  Heavy rain and flash flooding in Bangkok early Monday also hit trade as many market participants struggled to get into work, traders added. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the government bond due June 2009 finished in a bid-offer range of 3.21%-3.20%, compared from 3.21% at Friday's close. The yield on the government bond due April 2010 finished at 3.52%-3.51%, little changed from 3.52% previously. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand sold THB6 billion in 28-day and 91-day treasury bills at an auction Monday as part of the government's regular deficit financing. \par \par \uc2  As expected, average yields for both maturities were softer from those recorded at last week's auction thanks to easing short-term interest rates and small offering sizes, coupled with liquidity driven demand. \par \par \uc2  The THB1 billion 28-day T-bills were sold at 1.8700%, down from 1.90397% recorded last week, while THB5 billion 91-day T-bills were sold at 1.90194%, down from 1.93501%. \par \par \uc2  Thai short-term interest rates slightly eased at the end of biweekly reserve requirements Monday. However, dealers said the rates may move up at the start of a new reserve period Tuesday, coupled with weakness in the baht. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term                   Monday      Friday\par Interbank  Overnight/Most Active  1.81250%    2.00000%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close        1.53125%    1.59375%\par            14-Day                 2.00000%    2.00000%\par            1-Month              no activity   no activity\par            3-Month              no activity   no activity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 662-266-0744; rattaphol.onsanit@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021007dya7000la\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
