\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Steady, suspicious of stronger yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2002\par 05:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were flat on Monday morning, waiting on key economic releases and political announcements from Japan but clearly unimpressed by the yen's gains. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unmoved believing the yen's gains would be short-lived, even as the dollar lost a yen from Friday's levels of 122.64/74. \par \par \uc2  Some attributed that to position-squaring after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixing on Monday, the last day of the fiscal first half in Japan, coupled with hopes the cabinet reshuffle will be pro-reform for the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan business sentiment survey due on Tuesday also prevented the market from being overly bullish about the yen's rally. \par \par \uc2  "No one's doing anything about the rising yen. They think even if the cabinet reshuffle is positive, Japan would want a weaker yen," a trader with a Singapore-based desk said. \par \par \uc2  The uncertainty over how Japan proposed to bail out its banks has increased the focus on the cabinet reshuffle due later in day, especially the fate of top financial regulator Hakuo Yanagisawa, whose opposition to the use of public funds to shore up banks has been criticised. \par \par \uc2  "Another volatile week, particularly for yen. Events and economic releases this week are especially difficult to predict and we expect the market to be extremely reactive," Lee Boon Keng, DBS Bank's senior strategist said in a market commentary. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar traded flat at 1.7775/7785 per dollar, barely changed from Friday and still close to last week's three-month low of 1.7872, while the Thai baht was little changed at 43.40/45. \par \par \uc2  The baht was near last week's five-month low of 43.60, where traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to release August trade data later in the day and economists expect strong export growth to help it show a surplus once again, after a deficit in July. \par \par \uc2  Ray Farris, strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston, said maintaining competitive exchange rates would still be a priority for Asian central banks. \par \par \uc2  "Over the next few months, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be dominated by swings in the yen and a preference by central banks to be averse to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, as long as leading indicators for exports are still deteriorating," Farris said. \par \par \uc2  Fragile stock markets, reacting to the fall in U.S. equities for the fifth week, also put pressure on some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Korean won , which has been plagued by rising foreign investment outflows in recent weeks, fell to 1,228.6/9 after a firmer opening. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was also weaker as its stock market fell, choosing not to follow the yen, while the Philippine peso hovered around last week's close at 52.35/45, just shy of 11-month lows beyond 52.50. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0310 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Japan yen     121.51       122.43         +0.76\par   Sing dlr      1.7776       1.7775         -0.01\par   Taiwan dlr    34.935       34.921         -0.04\par   Korean won   