\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late On Yen;Mkt Ignores Econ Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2002\par 11:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank to multi-month lows in tandem with the yen late Monday after a top Japanese official late last week suggested massive yen selling as a way to end Japan's deflation. \par \par \uc2  With Japan's financial markets closed for a public holiday, thin trading volumes made it easy to move prices as stop-loss buying of dollars was triggered across-the-board. \par \par \uc2  Market players ignored a slew of strong economic data from the region, choosing instead to track the yen's weakness which spilt through from Friday's global session. \par \par \uc2  The yen began to weaken against the dollar after Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, Haruhiko Kuroda, late Friday made his remarks about selling yen to combat deflation. \par \par \uc2  "It's triggered a lot of stop losses. Every \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved substantially (lower)" noted a senior trader at an Asian bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  However, the fundamental story hasn't changed, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the U.S. economy, ever-increasing prospects of a war against Iraq, and rising oil prices do not warrant a strong dollar outlook, they said. \par \par \uc2  Still, economic data from the U.S. so far, although mixed, indicates the U.S. economic recovery is on a recovery path. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, there's little to inspire confidence about the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's comments suggests that any serious policy steps to fight deflation appears to include a weak yen scenario, and it is that which is dragging down the rest of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "With an entire range of conflicting issues in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, direction recently has been a bit of blur, but if at all there is any focus, it is on relative performance and on this count, the USD may enjoy precedence over other majors," UBS Warburg wrote in a note. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won hit a three-month closing low against the dollar on the back of the yen's decline. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,220.0 won - its strongest since June 20 - from KRW1,203.8 Friday. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded between KRW1,212.0 and KRW1,220.5, breaking out of a nearly month-long trading band of between KRW1,190 and KRW1,210. \par \par \uc2  "Finally, it broke through its tight range," said Seung Hyun Kim, a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  corporate sales team at BNP Paribas. "We were surprised at this strong upward movement (of the dollar) today. I'm not sure if this trend will continue or not." \par \par \uc2  However, aggressive dollar sales by exporters supported the won, and this is expected to persist for the rest of the week. "Many exporting companies want to sell dollars over KRW1,220," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, the local dollar closed at its weakest level since Apr. 25 in line with the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  Importers bought U.S. dollars earlier in the day at around NT$34.71-NT$34.72, but buying of the local dollars by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and also exporters, helped cushion the local unit's fall. \par \par \uc2  However, offshore non-deliverable forward players started to cover their short U.S. dollar positions actively in the afternoon, pushing the U.S. dollar to its intraday high of NT$34.779, according to dealers. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit ended at NT$34.776, up from NT$34.410 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso closed at its lowest in 13 months, with the market jittery over mounting tensions in the Middle East, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at PHP52.44 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP52.23 Friday, and its highest since Aug. 9, 2001. \par \par \uc2  A trader with a local bank said there was talk in the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $30 million to support the peso early Monday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied this. \par \par \uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't see any need to support the peso as the decline wasn't caused by speculation, but by weaker regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  led by the drop of the Japanese yen, and mounting tensions in the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  "We feel there's enough liquidity. If there's excessive volatility, we will come in," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  He said the dollar is expected to slide back to just above 50 pesos near the year-end, with remittances from overseas Filipino workers expected to increase in the fourth quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar tracked the yen's weakness, briefly hitting a three-month low against the U.S. unit earlier in the session before profit-taking set it. \par \par \uc2  The market ignored news that the city state's retail index rose for the first time year-on-year after declining for eight straight months. \par \par \uc2  The index climbed 1.7% on year in July on the back of higher sales of vehicles and household equipment, the Department of Statistics said Monday. Compared with June, the index rose 6.9%. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened at around S$1.7712, and rose to an intraday high of S$1.7810 at mid morning - its highest since mid-June - boosted by fresh purchase orders from European banks, a trader at a big foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said: "The pair hasn't moved for quite some time. Now that the (U.S.) dollar is gaining on all its major counterparts, traders feel it's time (to see some major movements)." \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around S$1.7806, from S$1.7641 late in Asia Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the dollar's rise against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted offshore players to cover short dollar positions against the rupiah, a dealer at a Jakarta-based foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Bank Indonesia was believed to have sold dollars to help support the rupiah, but it had little impact as the amount was small and the selling was done late in the trading session. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom told reporters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was monitoring the rupiah's weakening against the dollar and may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary. \par \par \uc2  She attributed the rupiah's weakness mainly to lower regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uncertainty about a possible U.S.-led strike against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "If the rupiah keeps falling, we may do something, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said, but didn't say if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was active in the market Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 9,065 rupiah, up from IDR8,965 Friday, after trading between IDR9,020 and IDR9,070 intraday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, market players ignored better-than-expected second-quarter gross domestic product data from Thailand, with the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling past the 43-level against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's GDP rose 5.1% on year in the second quarter. A Dow Jones Newswires poll had earlier predicted an average 4.6% rise. \par \par \uc2  Chakramon Phasukavanich, secretary-general of the National Economic & Social Development Board, told a news conference the agency's 2002 GDP growth forecast has been revised to a range of 4% to 4.5%, from an earlier 3.5% to 4%, due to an improvement in exports and investment over the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  But it had little impact on the baht, which a trader said dipped to its lowest since May. \par \par \uc2  "The market is too busy following the rest of the (regional) \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, adding that there was rumor the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session to stem the baht's decline. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around 43.225. baht, from THB42.695 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020916dy9g000jf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
