\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Focus On US Factors, Iraq \b0\par\par\uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2002\par 12:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a mixed bag late Thursday after the Sept. 11 anniversary of the terrorist attacks on the U.S. passed without incident. \par \par \uc2  But as in recent days, few market players took strong positions due to concerns over a possible war with Iraq, and uncertainty about the global economy. Any attack on Iraq would lead to higher oil prices, which would put a recovery under threat. \par \par \uc2  "People are looking for direction from the majors (\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), U.S. growth and Iraq," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Traders said market players were focusing on U.S. President George W. Bush's speech to the U.N. later in the day for clues of a timeframe for any such attack on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  They are also hoping U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan will throw more light on the state of the U.S. economy when he testifies before the House Budget Committee later Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar once again sank to its lowest level since late-May, closely tracking the yen's overnight losses. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso lost ground in late trading on a pickup in corporate demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah fell as local companies sought dollars to repay debt and fund imports. Some analysts said the rupiah was also weighed down by security threats. \par \par \uc2  But the South Korean won was lifted by exporters' dollar sales, while rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped keep the baht quite steady. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was volatile within a narrow range, veering between losses and gains against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Across the board, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "like a yo-yo," a senior trader at an Asian bank in Singapore said. "The market is adjusting itself. It was slightly long dollars (previously). So there's a bit of dollar selling today," he added. \par \par \uc2  "Volumes are not big; nobody has come in in a big way, but it's (the market) been active." \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended higher against its Taiwan counterpart for the fourth straight session as foreign banks bought the U.S. unit following the yen's overnight losses. \par \par \uc2  The gains were partly trimmed as some of the banks reversed course and sold the dollar after it weakened against the yen later in the session. Exporters were also keen to sell the dollar after its recent gains, a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar often tracks the yen because Taiwan and Japan compete in some export categories, and Taiwan also imports large amounts of goods from Japan. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.410 - another high since late-May - up from NT$34.395 Wednesday, after moving between NT$34.385 and NT$34.479 intraday. Dealings were valued at US$455 million, up from the previous day's US$371 million. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the dollar rose as local companies bid for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repay debt and fund imports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly sold small amounts of dollars at IDR8,895, but this didn't stop the U.S. unit from appreciating. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed the Asian session at 8,920 rupiah, up from IDR8,890 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan Chase's Malcolm said security and political concerns are also dogging the rupiah, and the already small number of players with exposure to Indonesia are unwinding short dollar positions against the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ignored a bomb scare at the British Embassy in Manila earlier Thursday after police found no explosives. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 51.970 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, from PHP51.910 previously, due to relatively higher corporate demand near the close of trading, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Intraday, the dollar moved between PHP51.840 and PHP51.990, with volume rising to $120.45 million from $72.5 million Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates are trigger-happy to buy dollars at levels below PHP51.90," said a trader with a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The peso's losses were limited by some banks unloading their excess dollar holdings. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, South Korean exporters continued their recent spree of dollar sales, helping the South Korean won stave off some of the impact of the yen's overnight fall. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at KRW1,201.4, down from KRW1,202.5 at the previous close, but the pair was confined to a tight band between KRW1,201.0 and KRW1,205.0. \par \par \uc2  "Frankly, this market is pretty hard to understand now because it's just stuck within a range," said a dealer in Seoul. "Nobody can find direction." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was choppy against its Singapore counterpart, moving up and down within a narrow trading band. Mainly, the Singapore dollar was tracking the yen's weaker tone, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened lower as traders took profit following its sharp overnight gains against the yen and euro. \par \par \uc2  But major foreign banks then started to build fresh long dollar positions around S$1.7564. \par \par \uc2  As of 1027 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted around $S1.7592, from S$1.7551 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar was quite steady against the Thai baht, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said to have stepped in to buy dollars at the THB42.39-THB42.40 level. \par \par \uc2  A trader at an Asian bank said the Bank of Thailand was rumored to have bought "quite a bit" of dollars -anything between $50 million and $100 million, he said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around THB42.522, from THB42.465 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020912dy9c000om\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
