\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Traders eye Wall Street sell-off. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Ovais Subhani \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2002\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, July 22 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed \par \par \uc2  in their recent ranges on Monday as traders focused on the \par \par \uc2  impact that Friday's sell-off on Wall Street will have on the \par \par \uc2  dollar and regional equity markets. \par \par \uc2      Dealers said a sell-off in Asian stocks in sympathy with \par \par \uc2  the U.S. stocks' fall to multiyear lows could potentially \par \par \uc2  reverse some of the recent gains regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had made \par \par \uc2  on the back of equity-related foreign inflows. \par \par \uc2      "Wall Street fears reign. Markets around the region are \par \par \uc2  bracing for more sell-off in the U.S. stock market," said \par \par \uc2  Philip Wee, treasury market strategist at Singapore's DBS Bank. \par \par \uc2      "A defensive mood may also see Asian government's guarded \par \par \uc2  against further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," he added. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     On Friday, the Dow Jones industrial average        lost\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 4.64 percent, Nasdaq composite fell 2.79 and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par broader S&P 500 index plunged 3.84 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Dealers said with the yen        back to levels around 116\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par per dollar, off its Friday's high around 115.50, players would\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par be wary of testing the tolerance of their central banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The central banks of South Korea, Taiwan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  all\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week in response to their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par hitting multimonth highs against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Still few players expected to take long dollar positions,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par they added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     WON SHINES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Korean won         -2 touched a fresh 20-month high at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1,165.3, about half a percent higher from Friday's onshore\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par close of 1,170.6.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Dealers in Singapore said dollar sale by offshore players\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and exporters pushed the won higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The sale of 700 billion won worth of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stabilisation bonds later on Monday was expected to cap the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par won's rise, they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Finance Ministry also said it planned to raise the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ceiling, initially to one trillion won, for those bonds to stem\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the won's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The ceiling was previously set at five 5.0 trillion won\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ($4.27 billion) for 2002, out of which 3.4 trillion won worth\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par has already been issued.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     However, the neighbouring Taiwan dollar dug in its heels at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par about 33.18 per dollar, compared with 33.023 where the onshore\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market finished trade on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Singapore dollar        at around 1.7300 per dollar was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par off its 19-month high of 1.7275 per dollar hit on Friday as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par foreign funds were spotted trimming their short dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But dollar sales from some local corporates and interbank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par players around 1.7335 spotted early on was expected to keep the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par downside limited.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Thai baht rose to 40.35 per dollar, from late\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday's 40.46, helped by foreign funds' dollar sales in early\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par offshore trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, players were wary of more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after data on Friday showed foreign reserves shot\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par up $714 million to five-year high of $37.62 billion in the week\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to July 12.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Dealers attributed reserve rise to Bank of Thailand's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recent dollar purchases.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S.       $0233 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     116.10       115.97         -0.11\par  Sing dlr      1.7310       1.7302         -0.05\par  Taiwan dlr    33.018       33.023         +0.02\par  Korean won   1165.60      1170.60         +0.43\par  Baht           40.35        40.46         +0.27\par  Peso           50.33        50.38         +0.10\par  Rupiah       8785.00      8770.00         -0.17\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \