\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Thai reserves hit 5-year high on baht  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Johnson \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2002\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, July 19 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves jumped by nearly two percent to a five-year high last week, official data showed on Friday, as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars to slow a rise in the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand (BOT) figures showed official foreign reserves jumped by $714 million to $37.62 billion in the week to July 12, taking the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial war-chest to its highest level since April 1997. \par \par \uc2  The rise reflects steady buying by the BOT of dollars and sales of the baht, which has appreciated sharply as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen on world markets and as the Thai economy has expanded more quickly than expected this year. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hit 40.20 per dollar on July 16, its strongest level since July 2000. Since then, the baht has eased back but still stood at around 40.50 by 0930 GMT on Friday, well above its recent low of 45.87 per dollar seen almost exactly a year ago. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula confirmed this week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to restrain the rise in the Thai unit and limit its volatility. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official attributed the $714 million rise in the reserves last week partly to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The rise was partly due to dollar buying," the official, who declined to be identified, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "Part of the increase was also due to an upward mark-to-market of the value of other assets held as part of our reserves," she said, but declined to elaborate. \par \par \uc2  PRE-CRISIS LEVEL \par \par \uc2  Thailand's reserves - consisting of dollars, gold, special drawing rights with the International Monetary Fund and other foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - have risen by $4.6 billion this year and are now above their level before the Asian crisis of 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  The increase is partly a by-product of the Bank of Thailand's "managed float" policy for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the baht can move freely on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets but the Thai authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time to try to reduce its volatility. \par \par \uc2  But the rise in reserves also reflects a desire by the government of Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra to avoid a repeat of the crisis of 1997/98, which saw the baht crash to a low of 56.9 per dollar in early 1998 from 25 per dollar a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  The fall of the baht - caused by more than a decade of Thai current account deficits and heavy speculative selling by hedge funds as the Thai authorities attempted to peg the baht to the dollar - triggered a pan-Asian economic crisis and regional recession. \par \par \uc2  Several of the ministers of the current government, including Thaksin, were members of administrations just before the crisis. \par \par \uc2  General Chavalit Yongchaiyudh, now defence minister, was prime minister on July 2 when the Bank of Thailand finally gave up its defence of the baht and allowed it to plummet. \par \par \uc2  Economists say Thaksin's administration believes a high level of foreign reserves is essential to avoid another crisis - even though the Thai economy is now much stronger than it was five years ago and has been running healthy current account surpluses. \par \par \uc2  The Thai economy is likely to grow by around four percent in 2002, after an expansion of 1.8 percent in 2001, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The increase of almost $5 billion in foreign reserves has so far had little impact on the Thai economy, thanks partly to the sale of large volumes of Thai baht bonds. \par \par \uc2  "This (rise in the reserves) should have caused base money to rise 40 percent," Merrill Lynch Phatra Securities said in a note to clients this week. "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sterilized by an equivalent sale of the BOT's domestic assets. Base money has been kept more or less stable at 550 billion baht." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020719dy7j00l5p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
