\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Hits New High On Dollar Stop-loss Selling \b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2002\par 12:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht hit a new 22-month high at the close of Asian spot trade Tuesday, due to stop-loss dollar selling after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below key support, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended the bumpy day at its intraday low at 41.30 baht, down from THB41.45 at Asian markets close Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened around THB41.40 as offshore players continued to sell the dollar, taking their cue from its overnight weakness against the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  "The greenback dropped to THB41.33 in the morning, but then staged a brisk rebound to THB41.41 within 10 or 20 minutes," said a trader at a foreign bank, "A U.S. bank seemed to be behind that quick swing." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the move appeared to be driven by short-term speculation and didn't seem to be an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which usually sets a bid before it starts buying. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been occasionally buying dollars to build its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and slow the baht's appreciation against the dollar. It usually acts through certain Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  As the sudden demand petered out, the dollar resumed its weakening trend in the afternoon, fueled by the yen's strength on demand from European markets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded between Y118.35 and Y118.95 on the spot market Tuesday morning in Asia - near its recent nine-month low against the yen - on ongoing concerns about the accuracy of U.S. corporate accounts. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit hovered around THB41.35 in Thai afternoon trade, as the dollar-yen managed to hold above Y118.50. \par \par \uc2  However, volatility erupted again as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost its support against the Thai unit on profit-taking by those who collected the U.S. unit earlier in the day, a trader said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's slip below THB41.35 also ignited stop-loss selling that sent it to the intraday low," he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders expected the U.S. unit to move between THB41.25 and THB41.40 when Asian markets open Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Thai government bond prices ended higher Tuesday as institutional investors returned to buy medium to long-term papers after their recent falls, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bond market has been bullish since concerns over new supply in the secondary market from the Financial Institutions Development Fund, or FIDF, disappeared. The plan is to issue savings bonds, which won't be traded on the secondary market. \par \par \uc2  Gains in U.S. Treasurys Monday and expectations that U.S. interest rates will stay flat this year also helped boost sentiment, traders added. \par \par \uc2  Goldman Sachs has pushed back its view on when it expects the next interest rate hike by the U.S. Federal Reserve to the second half of 2003 from November, citing a recent slowing in real consumer spending growth, weak equity prices and a recent Fed study suggesting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should err on the side of over-accomodation. \par \par \uc2  "Funds stepped in to snap up the bonds after their weakness late last week. The market sentiment is very good, so there is no reason to sell at the moment. Investors just wait for a good time to buy," a local bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Yields dropped five basis points from Monday. Yields moves in the opposite direction to prices. \par \par \uc2  The bond maturing December 2008 closed in a bid-offer range of 4.19%-4.18% versus 4.25% at Monday's close, while the April 2014 issue finished at 4.80%-4.78%, compared with 4.85% at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry has sold at an auction THB1 billion of 10-year state enterprise bonds to be issued by the Expressway & Rapid Transit Authority of Thailand, or ETA, at a yield of 5.340%. \par \par \uc2  The ETA bond yield was quite good, as expected, offering a nine basis point premium over the government bond of the same maturity. \par \par \uc2  Thai one-day repurchase interest rate dropped slightly as most banks were flush with liquidity. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term                  Tuesday         Monday\par Interbank  Overnight/Most Active 1.7500%         1.7500%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close       1.53125%        1.56250%\par            14-Day                2.0000%         2.0000%\par            1-Month             no activity     no activity\par            3-Month             no activity     no activity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 662-266-0744; rattaphol.onsanit@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020709dy79000l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
