\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Dlr Hits Multi-Month Lows \b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2002\par 12:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar sank to fresh multi-month lows against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday as news of yet another potential accounting scandal in the U.S. fueled fears of more capital flight from U.S. markets. \par \par \uc2  Telecom giant WorldCom Inc. announced overnight that it had improperly booked $3.8 billion of expenses as capital expenditures over the past five quarters, obliging it to restate its earnings. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the pressure on the dollar in global markets were comments by President George W. Bush and Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill seemingly endorsing a weaker U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Both spoke of the dollar's recent movements reflecting market forces. But perhaps most significant was Bush's additional reference implying that a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help the U.S. manufacturing sector. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will seek its level based upon market forces, and based on whether or not our country can rein in spending, can recover, can revitalize our manufacturing base," Bush said at a meeting of world leaders in Canada. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg said Bush's comment was "extremely bad news for the dollar as it represents a massive watering down - albeit implicitly - of the strong dollar policy." \par \par \uc2  Selling pressure against the dollar was so strong in Asia that aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to prop up the dollar did little to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a 21-month low against the Thai baht, an 18-month low against the South Korean won, a 13-month low against the New Taiwan dollar and a nine-month low against the Singapore dollar. The dollar also weakened against the Philippine peso, but strengthened against the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  WorldCom's announcement, following a string of accounting scandal's starting with Enron Corp.'s collapse last year, dealt another blow to faith in U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  Sharp declines in Nasdaq and S&P 500 stock futures in Asian trading promised heavy losses on Wall Street Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Yet another accounting scandal may make investors think twice about buying any U.S. stocks. That's bound to be bad for the dollar," Bank of America said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Against the won, the dollar closed at KRW1,203.9, down from KRW1,213.7 Tuesday. It was the won's strongest showing since December 2000. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy's reiteration that it is "deeply concerned" about the dollar's fall versus the won had only fleeting impact on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar's advance was restrained by forceful \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.622 versus NT$33.738 Tuesday. It was the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strongest finish since May 2001. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an estimated US$500 million to keep a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. Without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the U.S. dollar probably would have fallen through the NT$33.500 level, they added. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht benefited from the strength of its regional peers and comments from a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at THB41.740, down from THB41.900 late Tuesday. It was the baht's strongest finish since September 2000. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar took advantage of the broad U.S. dollar weakness to post its best performance since last September. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.7633, down from S$1.7719 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  A report showing better-than-expected industrial output performance for Singapore in May also pressured the U.S. dollar, though the data failed to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below a key support of S$1.7620. \par \par \uc2  The peso's gains were more muted due in part to ongoing investor concerns about the country's growing budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 50.35 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from Tuesday's PHP50.45. \par \par \uc2  "The peso should be trading below PHP50.00 by now if it weren't for concerns on the budget deficit and lack of dollar supply," a trader at local bank said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah failed to take advantage of the dollar's broad decline as local companies bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repay foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at IDR8,685, up from IDR8,675 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020626dy6q000oe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
