\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Ctrl Bk Gov: Baht Is Not Too Strong \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2002\par 07:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Tuesday that the baht's recent strength against the U.S. dollar hasn't been excessive relative to the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "When the dollar is weakening like this, it's impossible that the baht wouldn't move. Baht strength is due to market mechanisms," Pridiyathorn told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Since the baht has moved in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Thailand's regional trade partners and export competitors, its current level still supports exports, he said. \par \par \uc2  Against the U.S. dollar, the yen has strengthened 2.5%, the Singapore dollar 2%, while the baht has risen only 0.5%, Pridiyathorn said, without indicating the exact period. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was quoted at THB42.83 in early afternoon trade Tuesday, down from THB42.87 at the Asian open and THB43.065 at the Asian close Friday, in line with the U.S. unit's losses against the Japanese yen. Thai markets were closed for a public holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn's remarks had little effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have been regularly buying dollars on weakness this year to maintain foreign reserves while paying back loans from an expired International Monetary Fund rescue program. \par \par \uc2  The governor said previously that he doesn't consider such activity to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves rose $400 million in the week to April 26 to $34.6 billion, although part of the increase was probably due to revaluation of yen holdings. Under Pridiyathorn, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has ceased to provide explanations for movement in the country's foreign reserve levels. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Somkid Jatusripitak said separately Tuesday that Bank of Thailand should continue to monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so that the baht doesn't strengthen to the point where export competitiveness is weakened. \par \par \uc2  Somkid said current baht levels and the recent appreciation haven't hurt exports much so far, however. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Tuesday, Commerce Minister Adisai Bhodaramik said a good exchange rate range for the country's exports would be between THB43 and THB44 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The baht shouldn't be too strong, but it wouldn't be a problem if it doesn't stay too strong for too long, he said. \par \par \uc2  Adisai doesn't have any direct influence on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but his ministry is charged with increasing exports. \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand's poor export performance recently was caused by slow global demand, not by the baht's strength against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Thai exports were down 4.6% from the same month last year in March, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. \par \par \uc2  The March performance was an improvement over the last few months, however. Exports amounted to $5.56 billion, above $5 billion for the first time since November 2001 amid signs of recovery in global demand. \par \par \uc2  -By Suttinee Yuvejwattana and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; suttinee.yuvejwattana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020507dy57000ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
