\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Up Against Dollar; Tracking Firmer Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2002\par 12:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended higher against the U.S. dollar in Asian spot trade Thursday, tracking strength in the yen. But support for the dollar from local corporate demand and offshore short-dollar covering prevented the baht from matching the yen's sharp gains, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 43.225 baht, down from THB43.29 at the Asian close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit opened down in Bangkok from the previous close at THB43.255, after the dollar/yen slipped below Y130 overnight. \par \par \uc2  As the yen strengthened to under Y129 to the dollar during the Asian session, however, firm support was in turn provided to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at THB43.20 from local corporate demand and by squaring of short-dollar positions to take profits. \par \par \uc2  Some traders also suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent a sharp appreciation in the baht, which would hurt exporters. Others were skeptical though as Thursday's movements in the baht weren't out of line with the regional trend. \par \par \uc2  If the dollar/yen rate keeps heading south, dealers said the U.S. dollar could breach support at THB43.20 overnight. The dollar is expected to open in Asian trade Friday in a range of THB43.10 to THB43.25. \par \par \uc2  Thai government bond prices closed higher Thursday supported by demand due to the excess liquidity in the financial system and an improvement in global bond market sentiment on expectations that U.S. interest rates will remain stable for longer than was expected, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the government bond due December 2006 ended in a bid-offer range of 3.57%-3.55%, down from a previous close of 3.5925%, dealers said. The December 2008 issue finished at in a 4.48%-4.46% range, down from 4.50% previously. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry auctioned Thursday THB1 billion in government-guaranteed six-year state enterprise bonds to be issued May 8 by the Mass Rapid Transit Authority, or MRTA, at an accepted yield of 4.49%. \par \par \uc2  The yield represented a very low premium of about five basis points over the government bond yield curve as of Wednesday's close, or about eight-to-10 basis points over Thursday's closing yield curve. A low premium had been expected due to generally positive sentiment and liquidity driven demand. \par \par \uc2  Trade was very quiet Thursday after an initial rally. A foreign bank dealer said the pattern should be repeated Friday if U.S. bonds do well overnight. \par \par \uc2  If the U.S. bond market runs into profit-taking overnight, however, yields of Thai bonds will probably edge higher Friday, he said. \par \par \uc2  Money market interest rates ended mostly flat, and are expected to remain at these levels in the coming weeks due to excess liquidity. Money market rates should remain stable even if commercial banks cut deposit rates, as expected in the next week or so, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 14-day repurchase market interest rate has probably bottomed already at 2%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market      Term                 Thursday       Wednesday\par Interbank  Overnight/Most Active 1.9375%        1.9375%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close       1.6250%        1.6250%\par            14-Day                2.0000%        2.0000%\par            1-Month               2.0000%      no activity\par            3-Month             no activity    no activity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744