\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-Thai  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  to nullify baht volatility. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Johnson \par \uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 07:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, March 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  promised on Friday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when needed in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to iron out fluctuations in the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has risen about five percent against the dollar in recent months. \par \par \uc2  But Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula told Reuters the Bank of Thailand did not have a target level for the Thai baht, which now trades at about 43.40 per dollar, against 45.85 in the middle of last year and 25 before the Asian economic crisis began in 1997. \par \par \uc2  "There is a tendency for the baht to be strong and we have tried not to allow it to be too strong," Pridiyathorn said in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody in this world knows what level is healthy for the baht," he said. "Exchange rate is not an absolute thing, not an end in itself. It is a means to an end. It is a means for exporters to trade comfortably and conveniently." \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn said his officials entered the markets every now and then to support its policy of a "managed float" for the baht. \par \par \uc2  "Once in a while, we nullify, don't say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  "We manage for the glory of the country, for the convenience and comfort of exporters. We have to nullify the extra (capital) flows, the big amount of flows on certain days so it will not affect the exchange rate too much." \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn, who was a private banker, took over as governor in June 2001 after his predecessor fell out with the newly elected administration of Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra, largely over interest rates. \par \par \uc2  CAPITAL OUTFLOWS \par \par \uc2  Former Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul had cut Thailand's benchmark rate, the 14-day repurchase rate, to an historic low of 1.50 percent in an effort to stimulate the Thai economy at a time when its main trading partners were going into recession. \par \par \uc2  Exports account for almost 60 percent of Thailand gross national product (GDP) of around $120 billion. \par \par \uc2  But Thaksin's administration was alarmed by net outflows of capital in the first half of last year and the new governor quickly reversed the rate policy, pushing the 14-day repo rate up by a full percentage point to 2.50 percent despite a series of rate cuts by the United States and other major economies. \par \par \uc2  Since then the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut 50 basis points off the repo rate in two stages to 2.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn said the raising of rates last year had been necessary to bolster the country's foreign reserves and to slow capital outflows. But since then the situation had stabilised and foreign reserves had risen. \par \par \uc2  His aim now was to have foreign reserves at least twice the level of the country's short-term private foreign debt, which now stood at around $13 billion. Thailand's foreign reserves amounted to $33.6 billion as of March 15, Bank of Thailand data show. \par \par \uc2  The governor said he was satisfied with the current level of reserves and their ratio to short-term debt. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn declined to give his views of the trend for Thai interest rates, saying the bank's Monetary Policy Committee, which he chairs, would decide on the rate "democratically" depending on the future economic environment. \par \par \uc2  "BIG BROTHER" \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand's main monetary policy "framework" would remain the bank's inflation target of between zero and 3.5 percent. Thai consumer price headline inflation was just 0.3 percent year-on-year in February. \par \par \uc2  "We are scared of hyper-inflation and don't want deflation." \par \par \uc2  He repeated previous forecasts that Thai growth was on track for two to three percent growth this year and said the bank's growth forecast would be reviewed soon. \par \par \uc2  Thaksin forecast last week that Thai GDP growth would be "3.5 up" percent this year, after growth of 1.8 percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn said Thai exports would also recover this year and rejected suggestions by some economists that the recent strength of the baht was hurting sales of Thai products overseas. \par \par \uc2  Thailand saw an 8.4 percent year-on-year fall in exports in February, which pushed the dollar value of Thai exports to its lowest level in 32 months, commerce ministry figures show. \par \par \uc2  "Exports dropped because of world demand," he said. "It is not because of the (baht) policy. It is because of the real demand drop." \par \par \uc2  He said the key for the Thai economy for the rest of the year would be the performance of the United States economy. "The U.S. is the big brother - the big buyer in the world," he said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, he would maintain a cautious policy with strong reserves and a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , preparing for any "uncertainty". \par \par \uc2  "Buddhism says the only certain thing is uncertainty." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020329dy3t00bkz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
