\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late On Equity Inflows, Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 10:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Continuing foreign equity inflows into local stock markets and the yen's gains bolstered most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah's new found strength provided additional support to its Southeast Asian peers, although fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks in Singapore and \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  stymied their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rise. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah extended its climb toward IDR9,900 resistance, as interbank participants continued to sell U.S. dollars in anticipation of inflows from the Indonesian government's impending sale of its 51% stake in PT Bank Central Asia, observers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, trading at near five-month highs, hit an intraday high of IDR9,915 before retreating to around IDR9,950 late Wednesday. The rupiah was weaker at IDR9,980 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Improving prospects for the privatization of state-held assets in Indonesia - which would underscore the government's commitment to economic reforms - helped thrust the rupiah through the psychologically important IDR10,000 threshold Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "With corporate bids for debt repayment still much on the sidelines, the rupiah looks well positioned to test IDR9,800," Bank of America said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to an intraday high of 43.490 baht to the dollar, thanks to strong foreign equity inflows and dollar sales by U.S. funds - as they simultaneously unloaded their dollar positions against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the baht subsequently pulled back past the THB43.500 level on market suspicions that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought dollars via two of its agent banks based in Singapore to check the baht's advance, market observers said. \par \par \uc2  In an apparent bid to maintain the price competitiveness of Thailand's exports, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been buying dollars in the last few days when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at THB43.55-THB43.60, said a dealer at a Thai bank. \par \par \uc2  "It's still in their practice to protect their exporters," he added. \par \par \uc2  At 0915 GMT (4:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was at THB43.515, lower than THB43.665 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was quoted at Y131.84, below Y132.13 in New York and Y132.53 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday provided a floor for the U.S. dollar around S$1.8250, observers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar slid to an overnight low of S$1.8222 on long-liquidation, but subsequently bounced back on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS, some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A large Singapore bank bought a "significant amount" of U.S. dollars around S$1.8260 during the Asian session Wednesday, moderating the upward pressure on the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the firmer yen and local stock market gains, said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Although some dealers said the Singapore bank was buying U.S. dollars on behalf of its corporate clients, jitters about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  led some to speculate that the Singapore bank was acting at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won closed at KRW1,316.3 against the dollar, little changed compared with KRW1,316.4 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The won strengthened to an intraday high of KRW1,313.7 earlier, before retreating as foreign investors turned net sellers of South Korean shares and onshore demand for dollars emerged when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded below KRW1,315 support, said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  State-run banks, like Korea Development Bank, or KDB, were snapping up dollars on behalf of corporations and oil importers, said a dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Some market participants also believe that South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through KDB, he added. \par \par \uc2  Expectations that the yen will remain rangebound - as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants attempt to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options positions - also limited the won's upside, said Mohi-uddin. \par \par \uc2  Market participants this week established "double no-touch options with barriers at Y131.50 and Y135.50," the strategist said, adding that these players will protect these levels to ensure that the options won't be exercised. \par \par \uc2  "External factors are not that bullish for the won," said Mohi-uddin. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar closed stronger on foreign equity inflows, but was off its intraday high as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A Taipei-based dealer estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for about half of Wednesday's turnover. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$35.030, slightly lower than NT$35.069 Tuesday. The New Taiwan dollar hit an intraday high of NT$34.995 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was US$602 million, higher than US$458 million Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Buoyed by its regional counterparts, the Philippine peso closed at\par PHP51.090 to the dollar, compared with PHP51.160 Tuesday.\par    -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020306dy36000le\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
