\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late On Yen; Focus On G7 Mtg \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2002\par 11:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were slightly lower late Friday, weighed by a tottering yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, with major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  providing little in the way of any solid direction ahead of this weekend's meeting of central bankers and finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were wedged within narrow ranges, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Participants will be looking out for any comments regarding the yen and Japan's economic and structural reform agenda at the meeting, to be held in Ottawa. \par \par \uc2  At 1030 GMT (5:30 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y133.95, above Y133.56 in New York late Thursday. The dollar was at Y134.05 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Trading was also subdued as participants refrained from taking any major positions ahead of next week's Lunar New Year celebrations, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar grabbed the limelight as it slumped to its weakest level in more than three weeks. \par \par \uc2  With financial markets in Taipei closed from Monday through Thursday, continued uncertainty over the wobbly yen's fate prompted local interbank traders, importers and offshore non-deliverable forward participants to snap up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "As downside risks for the yen remain, many would prefer hedging their positions in U.S. dollars," said a Taipei-based dealer. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended at NT$35.080 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , weaker compared with NT$35.029 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which had recently been buying U.S. dollars to curb the appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - turned sellers of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday to check the New Taiwan dollar's decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to 43.925 baht to the dollar, compared with THB43.855 late Thursday, in part due to fears Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "BOT was rumored to have taken $50 million off the market below THB43.90 on Thursday," Bank of America said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand said Friday that its foreign reserves had climbed to $34.0 billion as of Feb. 1, from $33.6 billion a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  The increase in reserves reinforced the market's belief that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying dollars to prevent the baht from strengthening excessively, amid strong foreign portfolio inflows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying dollars," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the dollar closed at 1,320.2 won, up from Thursday's close at KRW1,318.2, bolstered by demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sales by exporters, however, capped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, they added. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to S$1.8347 against its U.S. counterpart, compared with S$1.8326 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar closed at 51.220 pesos, up from PHP51.185 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the region's trend, the rupiah was stronger, thanks to heavy dollar sales by state banks, believed to be acting on behalf of the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency, observers said. Dollar demand by corporations, however, thwarted further gains by the rupiah, they added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 10,250 rupiah, lower than IDR10,290 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020208dy28000mm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
