\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Ctrl Bk Fears Cap Gains \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2002\par 09:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Concerns that the region's monetary authorities may step in and smother the appreciation of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  moderated the gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Wednesday, even as the yen bounced past the psychologically important Y133 threshold to the dollar, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  The anemic performance of U.S., and in turn regional stock markets, also offset any potential boost coming from the yen, observers said. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average skidded 2.5% Tuesday amid fears that U.S. companies could fall victim to accounting problems like those that caused energy trading company Enron to go bankrupt. \par \par \uc2  "In the last few weeks there was a tug-of-war between a strong dollar/yen and equity inflows in Asia," said Jimmy Koh, head of treasury research at Singapore's UOB Group. "Now it's the other way around." \par \par \uc2  Portfolio inflows into the region's stock markets had helped cushion the blow on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealt by the weakness in the yen and the euro in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  With Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  having strengthened against their major trading counterparts, the region's authorities may now be anxious about keeping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  competitive, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained supported above the psychologically important S$1.8300 level, as the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  loomed, observers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar slipped to an intraday low of S$1.8305, but had recovered to S$1.8326 at 0840 GMT (3:40 a.m. EST). The U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8325 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The trade-weighted Singapore dollar is trading at the stronger end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy band, analysts said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore - which uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than interest rates as its primary monetary policy tool - guides the local dollar within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and key competitors. \par \par \uc2  "The Singapore dollar has been hugging the strongest end of our estimate of the band," said Koh at UOB. "In fact, it has breached it slightly." \par \par \uc2  Koh estimates the upper boundary of the band at around S$1.8340, based on the assumption that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has - earlier this month - narrowed the policy band to 1.5% on each side of the pivot point. \par \par \uc2  Although there were rumors in the last two days that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strength of the Singapore dollar, some observers believe the MAS will unlikely stand in the way of a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They argue that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be wary of setting a psychological ceiling on the Singapore dollar, as the yen is widely expected to strengthen further on fund repatriation by Japanese institutional investors before the fiscal year-end in March. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was quoted at Y132.55, below Y133.31 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers also blamed the U.S. dollar's pullback against some of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Singapore dollar and the South Korean won on long-liquidation of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the Federal Reserve's decision on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  After Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan's encouraging assessment of the U.S. economy last week, markets have priced in expectations that the Fed will leave short-term interest rates unchanged when it concludes its two-day policy meeting later Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign equity outflows countered the upward pressure on the South Korean won from the yen's advance, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors unloaded a net KRW200.1 billion in shares Wednesday, contributing to a 3.2% plunge in the benchmark stock index, and in turn the won's slide to as low as KRW1,319.0 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  However, the yen's rebound and mounting expectations that South Korea's sovereign ratings might be upgraded - probably sooner rather than later - overwhelmed the yen's influence. \par \par \uc2  Reinforcing such optimism was an announcement by South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy Wednesday that a team of analysts from Moody's Investors Service Inc. will visit South Korea Feb. 25-28 for an annual review on the country's sovereign rating. In November, Moody's revised the outlook on South Korea's Baa2 ratings to positive from stable. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at KRW1,311.0, lower than KRW1,313.6 Tuesday. The U.S. dollar fluctuated between KRW1,310.5 and KRW1,319.0. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea kept the U.S. dollar buoyed above KRW1,310 support. \par \par \uc2  "There's a risk that if it breaks that level, people will sell the dollar down to KRW1,290," said a dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Moderate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept a rein on the local dollar's rise, dealers said. Apart from the yen, the New Taiwan dollar received a boost from heightened seasonal demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the month-end and the Chinese New Year festivities around mid-February. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.985, down from NT$35.001 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was marginally firmer at 44.025 baht to the U.S. dollar, compared with THB44.040 late Tuesday. Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which dealers believe had been buying U.S. dollars in the last two days - kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyed above THB44.000. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the trend in the region, the Philippine peso was slightly weaker as month-end corporate demand for U.S. dollars overpowered the support from the yen and foreign equity inflows into the local market. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at 51.22 pesos, slightly higher than PHP51.165 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's gains Tuesday proved to be short-lived as Indonesian companies took advantage of the U.S. dollar's pullback to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was at 10,325 rupiah, higher than IDR10,295 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said Tuesday the economic challenges facing Indonesia are becoming more difficult as the government struggles to deliver promised reforms in the corporate and banking sectors. \par \par \uc2  Still, the IMF's board still agreed to excuse Indonesia's failure to satisfy a couple of conditions of its $4.5-billion IMF loan program. \par \par \uc2  The fund announced late Monday its approval of a $341-million loan installment for Indonesia, contributing to the brief surge in the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020130dy1u000hx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
