\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Thai reserves suggest  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  selling baht. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Johnson \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2002\par 11:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Jan 18 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOT) appears to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks in an effort to depress the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , judging by data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The BOT Web site ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bot.or.th/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.bot.or.th}}}\uc2 ) says Thailand's foreign reserves jumped sharply to $33.5 billion in the week to January 11, up from around $33.0 billion during the previous three weeks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts to buy Thai baht were flat at $1.8 billion on January 11, unchanged from January 4. But they had fallen the previous week from $2.1 billion on December 28 and December 31. \par \par \uc2  Taken together, the figures suggest the bank either entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week to sell spot baht and buy dollars or had previously unwound some of its forward market positions by committing to sell Thai baht forward. \par \par \uc2  It could also have taken a combination of both positions. \par \par \uc2  A BOT official who declined to be identified told Reuters Thailand had made no significant debt repayments last week that could have affected the figures. \par \par \uc2  "The changes in the reserves can be attributed to our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealings in spot and forward markets," she said, giving no further details. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rise in reserves probably reflected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow appreciation of the baht, which is now not far off its highest level for 10 months. \par \par \uc2  The Thai authorities have said they aim to keep the baht stable relative to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of competitor economies and officials have privately expressed some concern in recent weeks that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated sharply. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at 43.93-43.96 to the dollar at 1025 GMT on Friday, after trading between 43.80 and 43.95 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen from a three-year low of 45.87 per dollar last July but is still nowhere near its level around 25 before the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in July 1997. \par \par \uc2  BUILDING UP NATIONAL RESERVES \par \par \uc2  "My guess is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market to smooth out the baht's fluctuation," said Thanawat Patchimkul, head of research at DBS Vickers Securities in Bangkok. \par \par \uc2  "The rise in our foreign reserves does no harm. It's been a well known BOT policy to build up national reserves which, at $33.5 billion, is at a sound level." \par \par \uc2  Asked why the BOT would want to restrain baht gains, he said: \par \par \uc2  "It's not a question of where the baht should stand. I think what the BOT wants more is a stable baht that will make it easier for Thai exporters to do business." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand data, closely watched by financial markets for indications of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, suggest the Thai authorities may have more room to adjust local interest rates than previously thought. \par \par \uc2  The BOT's Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) will meet on Monday to consider whether to cut its key reference 14-day repurchase rate, currently at 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Thailand held out against the global trend towards easier monetary policy last year and surprised markets in June by raising the repo rate by a full percentage point to 2.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said at the time it was worried about a possible speculative attack on the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the selling that forced the sharp devaluation of the baht in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Analysts suggest the Thai government is still worried about the risk of a possible sell-off and may delay any rate cut, at least until after the U.S. Federal Reserve makes its next move. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of economists this week forecast the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably keep rates on hold until early March when its MPC is next due to meet. (With additional reporting by Vithoon Amorn). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020118dy1i00kds\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
