\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Respite From Rangebound Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 945 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2002\par 11:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Wednesday as they paused for breath ahead of an expected relapse in the yen over the medium term, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Except for the Singapore dollar, which continued to grab the attention of foreign funds, the rest of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were wedged within narrow bands, with little in the way of direction from a rangebound yen. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese officials continued to try to placate their neighbors' growing anxieties over the yen's slide to three-year lows last week. Although the officials said Japanese authorities weren't attempting to guide the yen lower, they suggested that the finance ministry would tolerate a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated Wednesday that his ministry thinks \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels should be left to the market. Analysts believe Japan has no short-term policy option but to depreciate the yen in order to revive its recession-hit economy. \par \par \uc2  Although market players expect a further reprieve for the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term, they are likely to succumb to another bout of pressure if the yen resumes its recent fall, dealers said. But expectations of an economic rebound in Asia will mitigate the pain on the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflicted by a wobbly yen, they added. \par \par \uc2  "The risk for the dollar is still on the downside in the near term," said a senior dealer at a European bank. "But dollar/Asians will find a floor if dollar/yen stays above Y130.50." \par \par \uc2  At 0940 GMT (4:40 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y131.48, above Y131.18 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  As participants sought for further clues on the state of health of the U.S. economy, caution prevails ahead of the announcement later in the day of consumer price and industrial production data for December, business inventories for November and the Federal Reserve's Beige Book on regional economic activity. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar stabilized around S$1.8300 against its U.S. counterpart late Wednesday after surging Tuesday, as foreign funds and local banks appeared to unwound their long U.S. dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Demand for U.S. dollars by local participants and U.S. investment banks helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regain its footing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's sharp bounce over the past week also stoked fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , putting a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the Singapore dollar seemed to have also been helped by hopes that the pace of contraction in the city-state's non-oil domestic exports likely slowed in December due to improved overseas demand for electronic products, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  According to a Dow Jones Newswires survey of 10 economists, non-oil exports likely fell an average of 14.9% in December from a year ago, compared with contractions of 21.1% and 21.6% in November and October, respectively. \par \par \uc2  BNP Paribas said that, although based on technical charts, the U.S. dollar could extend its slide to as low as S$1.8070, fundamentals still point to a weaker Singapore dollar in the medium term. \par \par \uc2  "Inflation remains low and the risks to the economy from uncertain global developments remain," the bank said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said Wednesday in its macroeconomic review that it expects the island-nation's economy to continue contracting in the first half of 2002 from a year ago before rebounding into positive territory in the second half. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.8303 late Wednesday, off its morning low of around S$1.8270 and higher than S$1.8253 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After slipping on heavy foreign equity fund outflows, the South Korean won recouped its losses to end higher. The dollar's repeated failure to break above KRW1,320 prompted local banks to dump the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward the market's close, dealers explained. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,315.8 won, slightly lower than KRW1,316.9 Tuesday. It peaked at KRW1,319 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net sellers of South Korean shares for the seventh consecutive day, with Wednesday's net sales amounting to KRW133 billion. Seoul's benchmark stock index ended 1.1% lower at a year-trough of 710.95. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar had too earlier felt the pain of foreign equity fund outflows, sinking to as low as NT$35.063 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the New Taiwan dollar regain its footing to end little changed at NT$35.000 per U.S. dollar, dealers said. The greenback closed at NT$34.999 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar closed at 51.280 pesos, above PHP51.255 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The market remains on guard amid persistent rumors of moves to destabilize the government ahead of the first anniversary of former President Joseph Estrada's ouster this weekend. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady at 43.865 baht to the dollar, compared with THB43.870 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Participants were wary of pushing the baht higher amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. Dealers believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought dollars in the last few sessions to curb the baht's rise, in part to maintain the price competitiveness of the country's exports. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was slightly firmer at 10,375 rupiah to the dollar, compared with IDR10,390 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020116dy1g000km\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
