\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Thai newspapers January 11. \b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2002\par 03:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Jan 11 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in the Thai press on Friday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy. \par \par \uc2  TOP STORIES \par \par \uc2  - Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, due to stop in Bangkok on Friday as part of a regional tour, is keen to scrap "chequebook diplomacy" and make Southeast Asia an equal economic partner (NATION) \par \par \uc2  - Iraq said it will send an ambassador to Thailand in six months. Iraq has had no ambassador in Thailand for several years although diplomatic relations have been maintained (BANGKOK POST) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand's education minister and several school directors have voiced support for an interior ministry plan to carry out urine tests on school and university students for signs of drug abuse (NATION) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand's constitutional court has forced the Thai Motherland Party (Thin Thai), which has one member of parliament, to close after the party failed to file an annual report on time (NATION) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand and Laos have agreed on the construction of a river port in northern Thailand and roads linking towns in northern Thailand and Laos with Kunming in China's Yunnan province. The projects will be funded by the Asian Development Bank (NATION) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand will use lethal injections for prisoner executions from next year, replacing its current firing squad method (BANGKOK POST) \par \par \uc2  - The Thai government radio station took a political talk show off the air on Thursday after the presenter read out articles from a banned issue of the Far Eastern Economic Review magazine. Thai police halted distribution of the magazine after it printed an article about Thailand's King and Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra (BANGKOK POST) \par \par \uc2  BUSINESS \par \par \uc2  - An advisory body to Thailand's cabinet, the National Economic and Social Advisory Council, has said it will arrange a public hearing a proposed new licence fee structure for the Thai telecoms industry (NATION) \par \par \uc2  - A Thai Airways International committee will select a new president in February, the firm's chairman said (BUSINESS DAY) \par \par \uc2  - Thai subway operator Bangkok Metro has agreed to pay compensation of seven million baht ($159,000) a day for delays if it fails to deliver the trains and operating system by July 15 2003 (BANGKOK POST) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand's finance minister has called on a state-run debt restructuring body, the Thai Asset Management Corp (TAMC), to speed up its work with over one trillion baht of bad loans. The body aims to restructure 500 billion baht of bad loans this year (BANGKOK POST) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand has postponed indefinitely the signing of an agreement to buy electricity from neighbouring Laos' Nam Theun Two project (BANGKOK POST) \par \par \uc2  - Thai banks are expected to post improved results this year as a result of the finance ministry's plan to reduce loan reserves against credit losses from their best customers, a bank source said (BUSINESS DAY) \par \par \uc2  - Thailand's petroleum demand for the first 11 months of last year rose almost four percent year-on-year, state-owned PTT Plc said (BUSINESS DAY) \par \par \uc2  - Thai waste management firm General Environmental Conservation (GENCO) said its net profit for 2001 would be lower than the 100.6 billion baht recorded a year earlier (NATION) \par \par \uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year by buying dollars to limit the baht's volatility (NATION) \par \par \uc2  ** Looking for more information from local sources? Reuters Business Briefing has seven Thai sources including Bangkok Post and Business Day. For details of the product please call your local help desk \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 44.03 baht). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020111dy1b004bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
