\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Econ Angst Besieges Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 935 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2001\par 11:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  found respite late Thursday from the wobbly dollar, amid waning optimism that the U.S. recession would be brief, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  The gains in most of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were, however, constrained by concerns that a prolonged downturn in the U.S. economy would only spell further gloom for the export-dependent region, market watchers said. Indeed, late in the day, these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - especially the Singapore dollar - had come off their intraday highs. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve's pessimistic assessment of the economy in its Beige Book report added to the market's realization that the economy could only get worse, before getting better. \par \par \uc2  "Economic activity generally remained soft in October and the first half of November, with evidence of additional slowing in most regions outweighing signs of recovery in a few districts," the Fed's "beige book," a report on economic conditions throughout its 12 reserve districts, said. \par \par \uc2  The report appears to add to the likelihood Fed policy makers will trim its short-term interest-rate target, now at a 40-year low of 2%, when they meet Dec. 11. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, U.S. dollar sentiment has now turned a bit bearish," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  A string of benign U.S. economic data issued in recent weeks had fueled hopes that the recession there had bottomed, bolstering the dollar. But the market started reassessing its views this week, following a speech Tuesday by Federal Reserve Governor Laurence Meyer that appeared to leave the door open for more cuts, and a weak U.S. consumer confidence report. \par \par \uc2  "The question that the market needs to ask itself is not whether the (U.S.) recovery is real, but whether the rapid pace of the recovery that it appears to want to price in is real," DBS Bank said in a report. \par \par \uc2  The steep falls in U.S. stocks Wednesday - which was in part roiled by Dynegy Inc's decision to pull out of merger talks with debt-laden Enron Corp. - also cast a shadow on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earlier fall against the Japanese yen and the euro worked in favor of the Singapore dollar, which briefly strengthened past S$1.8300 resistance to its U.S. counterpart earlier in the day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0930 GMT, the Singapore dollar was still firmer at S$1.8317 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with S$1.8332 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  After briefly falling below Y123 earlier, the dollar was quoted at Y123.19 late Thursday, above Y123.06 late Wednesday in New York but lower than Y123.55 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.8877, lower than $0.8884 late Wednesday in New York, but higher than $0.8845 in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won recouped its earlier losses to end stronger, as exporters, capitalizing on the dollar's gains, jumped in to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the last-minute for their month-end book balancing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,269.0 won, down from KRW1,272.1 Wednesday. The dollar had hit an intraday high of KRW1,276.2. \par \par \uc2  The won felt the pain from foreign equity outflows earlier in the day, which contributed to the main stock index's 0.5% slide, dealers said. Foreign investors unloaded a net KRW126.8 billion in shares Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying by some of Hynix Semiconductor's creditor banks also dealt a blow to the won earlier in the day, Seoul-based dealers said. The banks have dollar-denominated loans extended to Hynix and must buy dollars to boost their loan-loss reserves, they added. \par \par \uc2  Late in the afternoon, however, with the dollar steady at KRW1,274, exporters rushed into the market and unloaded dollars. \par \par \uc2  As the yen came off its intraday high, short-covering in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and intermittent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  countered the strength of the New Taiwan dollar, which was buoyed by moderate portfolio inflows and sales of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.465, marginally lower than Wednesday's close of NT$34.467. The U.S. dollar had touched an intraday low of NT$34.435. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue to keep the New Taiwan dollar steady ahead of Saturday's legislative election, which could herald the formation of a new coalition government. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the peso finished slightly weaker at 52.000 pesos on month-end corporate demand for dollars, compared with PHP51.965 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  With the market now focusing on the country's recovery prospects, the peso reacted with indifference to the Philippines' third-quarter economic report card - which was within expectations. \par \par \uc2  The Philippines' gross domestic product grew 2.9% in the third quarter, marginally higher than the government's target of 2.8% for this period. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a touch firmer at 44.050 baht to the dollar, compared with THB44.090 late Wednesday. Lingering fears that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might buy dollars around THB44.000 kept a rein on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, dealers said. Some dealers believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the baht's strength around that level this week. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was stronger around 10,400 rupiah against the dollar, compared with IDR10,450 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011129dxbt000lp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
