\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Dn Tracking Singapore Dollar In Thin Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 13:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht closed lower Friday in thin trade tracking the Singapore dollar after shrugging off data that showed a robust trade surplus in August, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 44.435 baht, up from THB44.385 baht at the Asian close Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit opened at THB44.43 and quickly rose to the day's high of THB44.47 on dollar buying by offshore players after the Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen, said traders. \par \par \uc2  Importers looking to square positions also bought dollars, but fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the baht limited the dollar's rise and kept it in a narrow range, they added. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyarthorn Devakula indicated earlier this week that he wouldn't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit excess volatility in the baht. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued trade data for August late Friday showing that exports contracted less than expected due to strong demand for agricultural products. Exports fell 7.6% on the year while imports slumped 16% on year, leading to a trade surplus of $729 million, the biggest in 17 months. \par \par \uc2  However, traders don't expect the trend to be sustained as exports are seasonally strong in August and external demand is expected to weaken sharply following the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht is expected to continue to trade in a tight range Monday and remain tied to movements in the yen and the Singapore dollar. Support is pegged at THB44.30 and resistance at THB44.50. \par \par \uc2  Thai government bond prices continued to rise Friday, supported by expectations that the Bank of Thailand may soon cut its key interest rate and hopes that the government may delay a large planned bond issue, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said interbank players actively bought short-to-medium bonds on the belief that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut its key interest rates if the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee cuts its Federal Funds rate at next Tuesday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  Another supportive factor was the possible delay of a planned issuance of THB320 billion government-guaranteed bonds, initially scheduled for the fourth quarter to finance losses incurred by the Financial Institutions Development Fund, or FIDF. \par \par \uc2  Officials earlier said the bond issuance schedule depends on parliamentary approval of the Public Debt Management Bill. The current parliamentary session ends late next month. \par \par \uc2  "They (investors) think that if the bill fails to pass the parliament in this session, the bond issuance may be delayed to next year," a local bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Yields, which move in the opposite direction of prices, on active bonds ended down by an average of seven basis points, after dipping around five basis points Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The most active issue, the bond maturing December 2006, closed at 5.05%, down from 5.14% at Thursday's close, according to the Thai Bond Dealing Center. \par \par \uc2  The June 2004 bond closed at 3.84%, from 3.91% Wednesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thai short-term money rates ended slightly higher as some banks\par reserved extra money for the weekend on uncertainties over possible U.S.\par retaliation after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the World Trade\par Center and Pentagon.\par Market      Term                      Friday    Thursday\par Interbank  Overnight/Most Active      2.7500%   2.6250%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close            2.2500%   2.1875%\par            14-Day                     2.5000%   2.5000%\par            1-Month                no activity  no activity\par            3-Month                no activity   2.7500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Supunnabul Suwannakij and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones\par Newswires; 662-266-0744; supunnabul.suwannakij@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010928dx9s000r4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
