\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Matching funds may not help Thai stock prices. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dominic Whiting \par \uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2001\par 08:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Aug 30 (Reuters) - Thai plans for using government money to lure foreign institutional investors into "matching funds" to help revive the country's flagging economy could help in the privatisation of state-owned companies, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  But they warn that the funds - which may initially be around $200 million - would be too small to significantly add to the momentum of an equities market that has already shown strong gains this year despite concerns over global economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra has recently visited several Asian countries to plug his government's proposal to match foreign investment with a 25 percent contribution. \par \par \uc2  Reports have suggested the Government of Singapore Investment Corp (GIC) and Brunei institutional investors could set up separate $100 million funds. Thailand says the Chinese government is also interested. \par \par \uc2  Details of the funds were scarce, but analysts suspect they could be used to smooth privatisation efforts for state-owned firms like the Petroleum Authority of Thailand (PTT) and Thai Airways. \par \par \uc2  One of the main perks for foreign investors in the funds could be first option on initial public offerings. \par \par \uc2  "There's a little fear with privatisations, that when they come up for bidding no-one will buy them," said Marc Lavoie, head of research at Asset Plus Management. \par \par \uc2  "But say if 80 percent of PTT has already been bought by Singapore or Brunei, people are going to jump in." \par \par \uc2  "I think the bulk of the (fund) money is aimed at privatisations or pre-privatisations. It's not an equity bailout fund, I don't think they're that stupid," he said. \par \par \uc2  THREE TYPES OF FUND \par \par \uc2  Thai officials said some funds would be for investment in the stock market and state-owned enterprises. But the emphasis would be on channelling money into cash-strapped private firms suffering from banks' ultra-cautious lending policies. \par \par \uc2  "The funds will be closed-ended, with a five-year limit, and there will be three investment types - portfolio in the stock market, direct investment depending on the objective of the fund, and investment for the privatisation programme," said Chaiyuth Sudthitanakorn, senior expert for fiscal and tax affairs at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The government thinks if we invest some money into potential, then companies can run and then list. Our economy can then move, not just stick." \par \par \uc2  Chaiyuth said Thai contributions could be from the government pension fund, state-owned Krung Thai Bank or the finance ministry, with most of any capital gains handed over to participating foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  "Our objective is not for profit, but we are in the process of asking foreign investors what they propose," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the funds would not be large enough to have any durable effect on share prices if they were put into a stock market which has already risen 25 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  In Asia, only China's recently liberalised hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B markets have outperformed Thai stocks in 2001. \par \par \uc2  "A DROP IN THE BUCKET" \par \par \uc2  "They're talking about $100 million each from Brunei and Singapore and trying to get the Chinese interested, but this is a drop in the bucket in equities," said Asset Plus's Lavoie. \par \par \uc2  He added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not copy the successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Hong Kong authorities when markets were jittery about the 1997 handover of sovereignty. \par \par \uc2  "It was successful (in Hong Kong) because no-one knew who was doing the buying, so everyone else piled in, and there was justification because stocks were undervalued," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe in Thailand there's not enough value because prices are up significantly and most sectors are no longer cheap." \par \par \uc2  But some dealers said investing in stocks could have a positive psychological effect. \par \par \uc2  "The absolute amount of money won't make an iota of difference, but the momentum from a perception that the government cares about the stock market will be a good thing," said Ben Knowles, head of sales at Seamico Securities. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said improved investor confidence would boost domestic consumption, a key goal of the Thaksin administration. \par \par \uc2  "Anything you can do to push up the stock market gets people excited, and excited people spend money," said Andrew Stotz, Vice President of SG Securities. \par \par \uc2  "We do need alternatives to bank deposits - because there's a huge mass of liquidity of 600 billion baht ($13.52 billion) - whether it's in equities, bonds or property," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010830dx8u00dqq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
