\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Vulnerable dollar cheers market. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2001\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 15 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were buoyed by a perky yen which rose to two-month highs against a backdrop of bearish sentiment towards the U.S. dollar and repatriation of funds to Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday was a big coupon maturity date in the U.S. bond market and repatriation of this flow was the principal source of the yen strength," said Ron Leven, chief Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But it is disturbing that these funds are not being rolled over in the U.S. market, and this is indicative of a world that is in risk reduction mode," he said, referring to the greater perceived risk surrounding the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Local factors also featured. The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent moves to squeeze peso out of the market by raising liquidity reserve requirements continued to boost the peso. \par \par \uc2  The market is focused on a Federal Reserve report due later on Wednesday on industrial production in July which is likely to show the 10th straight month of contraction in manufacturing. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was beginning to lose some of its shine, retreating below 8,500 per dollar partly on talk local banks were buying back dollars to meet foreign debt maturing in late August. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  source told Reuters a group of commercial banks would have to pay some $1.0 billion in restructured loans maturing later this month. \par \par \uc2  But the rupiah's downside continued to be underpinned by lingering hopes the new government would soon be able to convince the IMF to release stalled loans and the Paris Club to reschedule its debt. \par \par \uc2  The statistics bureau said GDP in the second quarter grew 3.52 percent on the year and 0.18 percent on the quarter, predicting growth would pick up in the third quarter because of optimism over the new leadership. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates headed north after two weeks of declines, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark one-month SBI fetching 17.30 percent at Wednesday's auction from 17.13 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso gapped up at the open to break through 51 per dollar for the first time since June 12, but dollar bids by corporates and importers pulled it off almost ten-week highs of 50.60. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said some interbank players were still looking to offload their stale long dollar positions on wariness over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves to push the peso higher. \par \par \uc2  Charts showed dollar/peso heavily overbought with the 14-day relative strength index reading at 11.31. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has squeezed peso liquidity through hikes in banks' liquidity requirements and has also penalised banks for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rule violations. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Department of Finance said spending cuts helped lower the budget deficit for January-July to 82.586 billion pesos, against a target of 93.586 billion. But revenues at 320.7 billion came in below the target of 327.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht ran into onshore commercial demand for the dollar prompting interbank players to cover their short positions. \par \par \uc2  But dealers in Singapore said offshore names continued to trim back on long dollar positions, keeping the baht in a tight 44.75-44.90 range. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wariness was also limiting gains above the session highs, around which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the baht's rise in early June. \par \par \uc2  Charts showed the dollar overbought against the baht with a 14-day RSI reading at 11.50. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar gave up some of its gains due to fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as players recognised its recent rise was excessive and not warranted by economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to 1.7520 in early trade before settling into a 1.7530-1.7570 range on talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) was in the market to check rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said its recent gains were fuelled by U.S. dollar weakness which set off rounds of Sing dollar short covering, but the longer-term direction for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be down. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is heavily oversold against the Sing dollar, with a 14-day RSI reading of 12.95. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar edged up, taking its cue from bullish sentiment in local stocks which ended at a five-week high. \par \par \uc2  But dealers in Singapore said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presence in the market kept trade subdued and within a tight range. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently been spotted capping the Taiwan dollar's rise to keep exports competitive. \par \par \uc2  Players were also cautious ahead of second quarter gross domestic product data due Friday. Most analysts expect negative growth for the first time in 26 years. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won nudged up in light, offshore trade as onshore markets were closed for Liberation Day. \par \par \uc2  The won was expected to take its cue from the yen when local markets resume trade on Thursday. A firmer yen has helped sentiment but selling of local stocks by foreign investors has weighed on the won in recent sessions. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0859 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     120.93       122.78         +1.53\par  Sing dlr      1.7544       1.7595         +0.29\par  Taiwan dlr    34.570       34.582         +0.03\par  Korean won   1274.00      1288.00         +1.10\par  Baht           44.83        44.94         +0.25\par  Peso           50.95        51.18         +0.44\par  Rupiah       8600.00      8470.00         -1.51\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     120.93       114.45         -5.36\par  Sing dlr      1.7544       1.7365         -1.02\par  Taiwan dlr    34.570       32.900         -4.83\par  Korean won   1274.00      1277.00         +0.24\par  Baht           44.83        43.70         -2.52\par  Peso           50.95        50.75         -0.39\par  Rupiah       8600.00      9470.00        +10.12\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010815dx8f00hwj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
