\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Higher Amid US Dlr Weakness Vs Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2001\par 13:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended firmer Wednesday amid U.S. dollar weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on concerns about the ailing U.S. economy and the sustainability of the strong U.S. dollar policy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at THB44.865, up from THB44.965 at the Asian close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over the U.S. economic downturn drove players to unwind long-dollar positions, sending the dollar to a two-month low at THB44.73 in early trade, as the baht played catch up to appreciation of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded in afternoon session to THB44.865 as offshore players bought back dollars in profit-taking, said a dealer at foreign bank based in Bangkok. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's low came after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Assistant Governor Chetthavee Charoenpitaks said there had been no unusual movement recently in the baht, which eased concern about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said baht's recent appreciation followed movement in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as weakness in the U.S. economy has put pressure on dollar sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The rebound in the dollar came as investors considered, however, that Chetthavee had declined to say at what level the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the baht strengthens too much. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in June when the dollar last weakened to around THB44.70. \par \par \uc2  Volume was active compared with light trade during the last few days, as offshore players squared dollar positions, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was expected to open Thursday in a range of THB44.70 to THB45, subject to the fluctuation of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. Next support would be THB44.50. \par \par \uc2  Thai government bond yields closed down in moderate trade Wednesday, led by buying from newly established funds, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Several new funds stepped in to buy some medium- and long-term bonds early morning to build up their portfolios, prompting other investors to follow suit," said a dealer at local bank. \par \par \uc2  The baht's recent strength also prompted some banks to invest in the bond market, she said. \par \par \uc2  Yields dropped by 10-20 basis points in early trade before late profit-taking emerged to limit gains, they said. The yields of most active issues closed down by 5-to-10 basis points. \par \par \uc2  The Thai Bond Dealing Center said in its daily report that the yield on the government bond due June 2004 closed at 4.660%, down from a last deal Tuesday at 4.70%, while the December 2006 issue closed at 5.79% versus 5.865%. \par \par \uc2  However, concerns over THB320 billion in government-guaranteed bonds to be issued in the fourth quarter by the Financial Institutions Development Fund continued to weigh on market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Some investors were also waiting to see the market's reaction to auctions of government bonds, which will resume next week. \par \par \uc2  Money market interest rates were mostly flat, on flush liquidity. The overnight repurchase market rate was down slightly as extra cash put into circulation ahead of the recent long weekend returned to financial institutions. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market      Term   