\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Higher Late; Baht Plays Catch Up \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2001\par 11:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained their firm undertone late Tuesday, showing limited reaction to the yen's pullback following Bank of Japan's surprise decision to further ease monetary policy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over Asia's tottering economies were pushed to the backburner, as position-unwinding continued to drive regional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, market watchers said. The severity of the U.S. economic malaise and doubts about the prospects for an imminent recovery have caught dollar bulls off guard, they added. \par \par \uc2  "They want to sell off their long dollar positions in line with the shifting perception of the dollar," said Thio Chin Loo, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  But "dollar bullishness is not over yet," she added. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar should be able to rebound once the recovery in the U.S. is evident in the fourth quarter," Thio said. \par \par \uc2  Markets are awaiting U.S. retail sales data for July, due to be issued at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The median estimate of 20 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for a 0.2% decline in total sales, but for a 0.1% increase when auto-dealer sales are excluded. \par \par \uc2  Until the dollar breaks out of its tight range against the yen, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also provide little in the way of direction for the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, observers said. \par \par \uc2  In response to a sharply deteriorating global economy, the BoJ said Tuesday it is to increase outright purchases of government bonds to Y600 billion a month from Y400 billion and to increase its current account deposits target to Y6 trillion from Y5 trillion, effectively flooding the financial system with more cash. \par \par \uc2  At 0900 GMT, the dollar was at Y122.75, higher than the Y122.41 late Monday in New York, but lower than its intraday high of Y123.15. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht finally snapped out of its recent ranges to play catch up with the current rally of its Southeast Asian peers. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged past 45 baht per dollar for the first time since early June, as offshore participants unwound their long dollar positions, dealers said. Stop-loss dollar sales were triggered just before the dollar's break below THB45, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 44.925 baht, down from THB45.135 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The baht will unlikely make much headway, as dollar demand, largely by onshore participants, is expected to emerge around THB44.80 to THB44.90, dealers said. They noted that bids from local corporates kept the dollar buoyed above THB44.80 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The market wasn't all that long in dollars (against the baht) because they were anticipating stringent reporting rules that would curb speculative baht trading," said Thio at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The revised reporting requirements for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions through nonresident bank accounts, unveiled last Thursday, turned out to be less stringent than earlier forms that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had come up with about a month earlier. \par \par \uc2  The rules will go fully into effect Oct. 1, following a transition period beginning Sept. 3. \par \par \uc2  While fears that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might buy dollars below THB45 somewhat restrained the baht's advance, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Tuesday the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended its rally to hit an 11-month high of 8,225 rupiah per dollar, enlivening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of most of its Southeast Asian neighbors, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has rallied more than 30% since President Megawati Sukarnoputri took power on July 23, following the impeachment of Abdurrahman Wahid. \par \par \uc2  Market hopes that her strong economic team may be able to resuscitate the struggling economy, and that the International Monetary Fund will soon resume a $5 billion loan program continued to propel the rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said Tuesday it will send a mission to Indonesia next week. \par \par \uc2  Continued dollar sales by the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave the rupiah an added boost, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But corporate dollar demand and rupiah profit-taking pulled the rupiah off its intraday high, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the dollar was at 8,455 rupiah, down from IDR8,560 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the baht and the rupiah combined with concerns over the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's watchful supervision over the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to shore up the peso to a two-month closing high, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 51.175 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP51.210 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was weaker, after a day of choppy trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was at S$1.7609, higher than S$1.7588 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won rose in line with the yen and as some market participants covered their short dollar positions or established long positions in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a local holiday Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,288.5 won, up from KRW1,285.5 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar received a fillip from a 1.5% rise on the local equity market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.582, down from Monday's close of NT$34.599. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper the appreciation of the New Taiwan dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010814dx8e000fa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
