\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Ctrl Bk Gov: Baht Stabilized; Hasn't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 123 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2001\par 08:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Tuesday that the baht has stabilized recently, and that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The economy and financial markets have stabilized, and foreign reserves are at a healthy level at $32 billion," he said, without elaborating further. \par \par \uc2  In early afternoon trade Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was trading just above 45.00 baht, off a low of THB44.92, in line with the yen's latest retreat. The baht has been tracking the yen and Singapore dollar recently, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; suttinee.yuvejwattana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010814dx8e0009j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
